  "final_message": "All tasks complete. Summary of what was done:\n\n**js/main.js** \u2014 implemented the Node.js ledger summator (the last missing language). Key design decisions:\n- `BigInt` for all monetary arithmetic (JS `Number` can't exactly represent int64 sums beyond 2^53), matching the spec's `usd = (hundredths * rate) // 1000000` integer floor division\n- Manual `parseAmount` converting amount strings to integer hundredths\n- Skips `type === \"void\"` and tags containing exactly `\"test\"` (case-sensitive)\n- Output: `counted N`, `net T`, channels sorted by name asc, top-10 accounts by net desc then id asc\n\n**Verification** \u2014 all five languages now pass s1/s2/s3 byte-for-byte, and on the new ~398MB / ~1.33M transaction benchmark (`bench/big.in`, generated via `gen_big.py`) all five produce **identical output**:\n\n| Language | Technique | Time |\n|----------|-----------|------|\n| C++ | custom byte scanner | 1413 ms |\n| Go | custom byte scanner | 2024 ms |\n| C# | Utf8JsonReader | 2871 ms |\n| Java | Jackson streaming | 4629 ms |\n| Node.js | JSON.parse + BigInt | 7775 ms |\n\nOne bug caught and fixed during validation: the JS account sort comparator was inverted (sorted ascending, picking the most-negative accounts). Corrected to descending by net, then ascending by id \u2014 after that, JS matched on all samples and the big input.",
